## 1. Terraform Meta-Arguments

Meta-arguments are special arguments in Terraform. They control how resources, modules, and other blocks behave.

The commonly used meta-arguments are:

| Meta-Argument | Purpose |
|---|---|
| count | Creates multiple resources |
| for_each | Creates resources using a set or map |
| depends_on | Defines dependencies between resources |
| provider | Selects a provider configuration |
| lifecycle | Controls resource lifecycle behavior |

## 2. depends_on

The `depends_on` meta-argument defines dependencies between resources.

It ensures that one resource is created or updated after another resource.

Example:

```hcl
resource "aws_eip" "myinstance_eip" {
  instance   = aws_instance.myinstance.id
  depends_on = [aws_instance.myinstance]
}
```

Here, the Elastic IP depends on the EC2 instance.

Commands:

```bash
terraform init
terraform apply --auto-approve
terraform destroy --auto-approve
```

## 3. count

The `count` meta-argument creates multiple instances of a resource.

Example:

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  count         = 2
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "WebServer-${count.index}"
  }
}
```

Terraform creates two EC2 instances:

- WebServer-0
- WebServer-1

### Using count with length()

We can use `length()` and `count` to create multiple EC2 instances with different instance types and names.

```hcl
variable "instance_type" {
  default = ["t2.micro", "t2.small", "t2.medium"]
}

variable "instance_name" {
  default = ["dev-server", "test-server", "prod-server"]
}

resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  count         = length(var.instance_type)
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = var.instance_type[count.index]

  tags = {
    Name = var.instance_name[count.index]
  }
}
```

## 4. for_each

The `for_each` meta-argument creates multiple resources using a set or map.

It provides more control when creating resources with different names and configurations.

### Example 1: Using toset()

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  for_each      = toset(["dev-server", "test-server", "prod-server"])
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = each.key
  }
}
```

Important points:

- `toset()` converts a list into a set.
- `each.key` represents the resource key.
- `each.value` represents the corresponding value.

### Example 2: Using a Map

```hcl
variable "instances" {
  type = map(string)

  default = {
    "web-server-1" = "t3.micro"
    "web-server-2" = "t3.small"
    "app-server-1" = "t3.medium"
    "db-server-1"  = "m5.large"
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  for_each      = var.instances
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = each.value

  tags = {
    Name = each.key
  }
}
```

### Example 3: Using Map of Objects

```hcl
variable "instances" {
  type = map(object({
    instance_type     = string
    availability_zone = string
  }))
}

resource "aws_instance" "servers" {
  for_each = var.instances

  ami               = var.ami_id
  instance_type     = each.value.instance_type
  availability_zone = each.value.availability_zone

  tags = {
    Name = each.key
  }
}
```

### count vs for_each

| count | for_each |
|---|---|
| Uses a numeric index | Uses a key |
| Uses count.index | Uses each.key and each.value |
| Useful for numbered resources | Useful for named resources |
| Resource instances are index-based | Resource instances are key-based |

## 5. Lifecycle Meta-Argument

Lifecycle controls how Terraform manages resource changes.

Important lifecycle settings:

1. ignore_changes
2. prevent_destroy
3. create_before_destroy

### ignore_changes

`ignore_changes` tells Terraform to ignore changes to selected resource attributes.

Example:

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "web_server" {
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "test-server"
  }

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [
      instance_type,
      tags
    ]
  }
}
```

Terraform ignores changes to the specified attributes during planning and applying.

We can also use:

```hcl
lifecycle {
  ignore_changes = all
}
```

This tells Terraform to ignore all changes to the resource's attributes.

### prevent_destroy

`prevent_destroy` prevents Terraform from destroying a resource while this lifecycle rule is present.

Example:

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "one" {
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "test-server"
  }

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}
```

If we execute:

```bash
terraform destroy
```

Terraform will block the destruction of this resource.

### create_before_destroy

`create_before_destroy` creates a replacement resource before destroying the existing resource.

It helps reduce downtime during resource replacement.

Example:

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "one" {
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "test-server"
  }

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}
```

When a change requires replacement, Terraform creates the new instance first and then destroys the old instance.

## 6. Terraform Providers

Providers allow Terraform to communicate with different platforms and services.

Three provider categories from the notes:

1. Official Providers – AWS, Azure, GCP.
2. Partner Providers – Oracle, Alibaba.
3. Community Providers – Maintained by individual contributors.

## 7. Terraform with GitHub Provider

Terraform can manage GitHub resources using the GitHub provider.

Example:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    github = {
      source = "integrations/github"
    }
  }
}

provider "github" {
  token = var.github_token
}

resource "github_repository" "example" {
  name        = "tf-github-repo"
  description = "Created using Terraform"

  visibility = "public"
}
```

Use a securely supplied GitHub Personal Access Token. Never hardcode or upload tokens to GitHub.

## 8. Terraform with Local Provider

The Local provider can create files on the local machine.

Example:

```hcl
resource "local_file" "one" {
  filename = "test.txt"
  content  = "This file is created using Terraform Local Provider"
}
```

Commands:

```bash
terraform init
terraform apply --auto-approve
ls
terraform destroy --auto-approve
```

## 9. Terraform with Docker

Terraform can manage Docker images and containers.

Example:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    docker = {
      source  = "kreuzwerker/docker"
      version = "4.7.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "docker" {
  host = "unix:///var/run/docker.sock"
}

resource "docker_image" "nginx" {
  name         = "nginx:latest"
  keep_locally = false
}

resource "docker_container" "nginx" {
  name  = "nginx-container"
  image = docker_image.nginx.image_id

  ports {
    internal = 80
    external = 8080
  }
}

output "container_name" {
  value = docker_container.nginx.name
}

output "container_id" {
  value = docker_container.nginx.id
}
```

Commands:

```bash
terraform init -upgrade
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
terraform destroy --auto-approve
```

Access the application using:

```text
http://<EC2-PUBLIC-IP>:8080
```

## 10. Terraform with Kubernetes

Terraform can manage Kubernetes resources using the Kubernetes provider.

First, create a Kubernetes cluster using Minikube or Kops and configure kubectl.

Example:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    kubernetes = {
      source  = "hashicorp/kubernetes"
      version = "3.0.1"
    }
  }
}

provider "kubernetes" {
  config_path = "~/.kube/config"
}

resource "kubernetes_deployment" "nginx" {
  metadata {
    name = "nginx-deployment"

    labels = {
      app = "nginx"
    }
  }

  spec {
    replicas = 2

    selector {
      match_labels = {
        app = "nginx"
      }
    }

    template {
      metadata {
        labels = {
          app = "nginx"
        }
      }

      spec {
        container {
          image = "nginx:latest"
          name  = "nginx"

          port {
            container_port = 80
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

resource "kubernetes_service" "nginx" {
  metadata {
    name = "nginx-service"
  }

  spec {
    selector = {
      app = "nginx"
    }

    port {
      protocol    = "TCP"
      port        = 80
      target_port = 80
    }

    type = "LoadBalancer"
  }
}
```

